**Read:** [Air-cleaning systems — DOE-HDBK-1169-2022](../sources/Air-Cleaning-Handbook-DOE.lingua#r_09QPEZ76E9W6HS7R55X2K8TB3X), flow distribution, test locations and instrumentation; [Demand-controlled cleanroom filtration — Faulkner, Fisk and Walton](../sources/Demand-Controlled-Cleanroom-Filtration-Faulkner-Fisk-Walton.lingua#r_2W1ADE030W9RFA8ATDKSYVT6XP), spatial observations and counter placement.

An average duct flow, a local velocity, a tracer response and a particle count describe different aspects of a system. A visible plume can reveal a path without measuring every relevant particle trajectory or proving chemical cleanliness.

Choose positions and times that challenge the proposed mechanism: behind an obstruction, during a transfer, near a return leak or at the sensitive surface. The test medium, equipment and intervention can alter the flow being observed.

**Work:** Design a comparison of two layouts using the same declared process state. Combine a flow measurement with a transport observation and a functional outcome. Explain which region remains unobserved. Carry measurement uncertainty and probe effects into the later qualification study.

**Return:** [Clean manufacturing and contamination control — from familiar objects to controlled processes](../Clean-manufacturing-and-contamination-control.lingua#r_7D8SQ8BVQDZQBDBGC6DP0JAMZN).
